Do not begin feature implementation until the audit package is complete and the owner has reviewed or explicitly authorized proceeding on its findings. Read-only inspection, baseline checks, and documenting findings are authorized by this prompt.

### 3.1 Establish the actual project boundary

1. Identify repository root, current branch/commit, working tree status, submodules/workspaces, application entry points, and any separate services/packages.
2. Read all applicable agent/project instructions and the relevant sections of the report and blueprint. Record versions, dates, locations, and whether each source was available in full.
3. Inventory languages, frameworks, runtimes, package managers, databases, queues, caches, hosting/deployment targets, market-data vendors, news/macro vendors, LLM providers, payment providers, observability stack, and current external integrations using repository evidence. Mark unknowns.
4. Record how to install, configure, run, build, lint/typecheck, test, migrate, and deploy according to existing docs/scripts. Do not assume these commands work.
5. Record relevant environment variables by name only; never print or copy secret values into artifacts.
6. Map trust boundaries and data flows, including browser/client, API, database, background jobs, provider calls, LLM calls, authentication, payments, and any broker-related code.

