## 9. Security, privacy, licensing, and data governance

### 9.1 Security and privacy

- Threat-model new endpoints, providers, LLM tools, portfolio/user data, exports, admin actions, scheduled jobs, webhooks and deployment secrets.
- Enforce authorization server-side at every request and job boundary. Test role matrix, tenant isolation, object-level access, privilege escalation and user deletion/export rules where relevant.
- Use secret stores/config mechanisms already approved. Never print, commit, embed in prompts, or log credentials, tokens, payment details, or sensitive provider payloads.
- Minimize data sent to third parties. Send no personally identifiable, portfolio, or account data to an LLM/provider unless the purpose, legal basis/consent where required, provider terms, retention and security have been reviewed and approved.
- Sanitize and bound untrusted external content. Treat headlines, web pages, files, prompts, comments, and provider responses as data, never as privileged instructions. Use strict tool allowlists; no model-generated arbitrary code, shell, URL, SQL, or order execution.
- Add rate, size, timeout, retry, and cost limits. Protect against SSRF, injection, denial of service, poisoned inputs and provider compromise as relevant.
- Log enough for audit and troubleshooting while redacting secrets and minimizing sensitive data. Define retention and access.

### 9.2 Data rights and quality

- Verify written/API terms, vendor contracts, user entitlements and display/redistribution rights for every price, macro, news, positioning, options, fundamentals, and historical dataset before shipping access or storage.
- Record attribution and any delayed-data notice, derived-data restrictions, retention limit, user/tenant scope, geographic restrictions, rate/cost limits and permitted display/export uses.
- No scraping, credential sharing, license circumvention, or redistribution unless explicitly authorized by applicable terms and owner.
- Do not store raw third-party content longer than permitted. Keep source references and permitted excerpts according to rights; design deletion/retention behavior.
- Validate units, currencies, contract rolls, timestamps, revisions, splits/adjustments if relevant, and missingness before calculations. Mark estimated/derived values as such.
- Define authoritative source and conflict resolution for each field; never silently merge incompatible feeds.

### 9.3 Financial and legal review boundary

- Flag jurisdiction-dependent financial product, investment advice, suitability, broker, payment, refund, consumer protection, privacy, market-data, and marketing claims for qualified owner/legal review.
- Do not claim compliance with any law, regulation, license or certification solely because code implements a control.
- Avoid unsupported performance marketing, “institutional-grade,” “AI predicts,” “guaranteed,” or similar claims. User-facing wording must reflect validated evidence and approved scope.
- No live execution or broker integration without a distinct written product and governance approval plus independent operational/security/legal review.

