## 11. Observability, reliability, and operations

Instrument changed paths using the repository’s existing tools. Define service-level indicators/targets only when approved and measurable; do not invent guarantees.

At minimum, where applicable, monitor:

- Data source success/error rate, freshness/age, missingness, schema violations, correction/revision rate, lag, and provider quota/cost.
- Advisory/forecast request volume, completion/failure/abstention/no-trade rates, stale-input rejection, processing latency, and missing citations.
- LLM provider/model availability, timeouts, token/cost budgets, output schema failures, unsupported claim/citation failure signals and safety rejection rates.
- Backtest duration, reproducibility/version completeness, invalid inputs and execution-simulation assumptions.
- Authorization denials, unusual access patterns, admin changes, export/deletion activity, and security errors without recording sensitive payloads.
- Database/queue/job health, backup status, restore evidence, error budgets if defined, and external dependency status.

For each alert define owner role, severity, trigger rationale, response steps, escalation, and safe degradation. Runbooks should cover stale or wrong prices, vendor outage/license suspension, corrupt historical data, model outage/cost spike, unauthorized access, bad release, and rollback. Never make a dashboard green by dropping events or hiding failures.

### Rollout and rollback

- Use existing environment/feature-flag patterns. Keep development, test, staging and production data/credentials separate.
- Default new financial intelligence features off or restricted until data rights, validation, security and acceptance gates pass.
- Release database changes in expand/migrate/contract steps when applicable. Verify old-version compatibility before destructive cleanup; destructive cleanup requires explicit owner approval.
- Define rollback trigger, operator, exact steps, data implications and verification. Prefer disabling the feature or reverting code while retaining compatible data.
- Preserve evidence and audit history during rollback; do not delete user decisions/forecasts to hide an issue.
- No production deploy, vendor spend, payment change, broker connectivity, or live order can be initiated without explicit authorization.

